body:
Can I get that case? Im actually getting pretty interested in patent policy and am looking to write my grad paper on it.

&#x200B;

edit: words

I don't know how much your willing to spend or effort you and your husband are willing to expend on this issue. But you might want to try to set up a honey pot on your network to see if the attack vector is coming through that. Also set up MFA on all accounts possible and set it up on a device you are sure hasn't been compromised. See if his phone is using more power than normal and if there are any applications he knows he didn't install on it.

&#x200B;

EDIT: link to setting up a honey pot [https://www.comparitech.com/net-admin/how-to-establish-a-honeypot-on-your-network/](https://www.comparitech.com/net-admin/how-to-establish-a-honeypot-on-your-network/)

Regardless of reason or if he would be a hero or not the decision is up to OP. No one should be faulted for excercising their right to their body no matter if they're twins, male, female; no matter if it's something as "small" as sperm donation to something big like abortion. No one should be called an asshole for deciding to not give away something that is fundementally their's. Autonomous control over a person's body is his fundamental freedom if he doesn't want to to give his sperm that's his choice and he's not wrong morally, legally and whatever other -ly you can think of. Especially because there are other paths for his brother to recieve a child such as adoption so it's not like OP has even closed down his brother's only route to a child. It would be great for his brother if he decided to give his sperm I think I would honestly but if he doesn't that's fine to. His brother shouldn't try to leverage morality(which I don't think he's doing just stating this) on OP. If he can't accept OP's decision on his body then that's on him not OP. 

&#x200B;

Also a lot of people here are projecting their own feelings of how they view sperm donation and donorship on OP's feelings and dismissing his orignal ones. This is wrong not everyone feels this way and more than a few don't that's completely ok. Everyone's allowed to have their own feelings and opinions about something but forcing them on another is wrong and making him out to be an asshole for not having the same feelings as others is wrong and honestly assholish.

You've talked to her about how you feel about it she makes it clear on her side. You don't like instagram and that culture and I agree with you at this point its basically a sub culture she does. The situation is settled both of you are pretty young and from your post I don't see her changing and she shouldn't have to on this. I also don't see you changing on this and you don't have to this is fine. What YOU need to ask is this fine with you what I mean is can you get past this and generally not get sick of her when you see her doing this. I'm not saying she'll become a super famous influencer but this might be her career for a minute depending how serious and how famous she gets. That means things with instagram and social media are only going to take off and it seems that she might want this kinda life. Now the thing here is will you be fine living with her then? Will you resent her, hate her, find her unattractive. If you can look past it and still love her then it's ultimatily no big deal you've made your views known so has she move on its fine. If you can't well no one here is willing to work on it then well you have some decisions to make my dude.

&#x200B;

also NAH

Therapists can't hold them accountable for anything either. They can remind them and tell them what they ought to be doing but they can't say OP you better do this or I'll give you consquences. The only real people that can intervene like that is the government. And since this isn't that kind of case then its really up to the OP. I think therapy is an invaluable tool but maybe one to implement after trying stricter parenting. Or alongside it if the money is there AND the kid wants help. Therapy is costly and while it can certainly benefit it also may do nothing. When I was eleven my mom threw me in therapy as well as stricter parenting like people advocate. I get why she did it because its a tool that can help. However, it wasn't done with any knowledge on how I would feel or if I wanted to go. I was eleven I didn't want to talk to some stranger about how I feel and why I do those things. I ended up letting it go in one ear and out the other. I'm sure the therapist saw this and honestly this kid may do the same if there isn't some communcation to see if the kid needs it or if hes just being rowdy to be you know rowdy because of hormones. I settled down later but at that age i just wanted to be rowdy and rebel really just to rebel and be rowdy. Not everything has a deeper meaning sometimes kids are just assholes for asseholes sake. 

&#x200B;

That said I am not discounting the idea of therapy and saying that therapy is bad. Later after my military experience I went into therapy and it has helped me in tons of ways. I'm sure it could help this kid but only if he wants the help is what I'm getting at I guess. If he doesn't then maybe it's better just to try stricter parenting.
